<header><p class="st-kicker">margraviate · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Margraviate of Austria</h1><p class="st-meta">976 CE – 1156 CE · capital Melk; later Vienna</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Babenberg march on the Danube, first named Ostarrîchi in 996, from which the Duchy of Austria and the Habsburg state grew.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After Otto I's victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lechfeld">Lechfeld</a> in 955, a march was established on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. In 976 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Otto II</a> gave it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-i-margrave-of-austria">Leopold of Babenberg</a>. A document of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-iii-holy-roman-emperor">Otto III</a> in 996 names the area 'Ostarrîchi', the earliest form of the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-babenberg">Babenberg</a> margraves pushed the frontier east to the March and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leitha">Leitha</a>, encouraging settlement and founding monasteries. Their seat moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melk">Melk</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulln">Tulln</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klosterneuburg">Klosterneuburg</a> and finally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> around 1145. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-iii-margrave-of-austria">Leopold III</a>, founder of Klosterneuburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heiligenkreuz-abbey">Heiligenkreuz</a>, was later canonised and became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria's</a> patron saint.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1156 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> raised the margraviate to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-austria">Duchy of Austria</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/privilegium-minus">Privilegium Minus</a>, compensating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-duke-of-austria">Henry II Jasomirgott</a> for giving up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. The new duchy, with special rights of inheritance, formed the nucleus of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-habsburg">Habsburg</a> power.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/margraviate-of-austria.jpg" alt="Margraviate of Austria" width="320" height="310"><figcaption>Image: Alphathon /ˈæɫfə.θɒn · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Austria_locator_map_(1000).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>976 CE – 1156 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>margraviate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Melk; later Vienna</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Leopold I of Austria, Leopold III, Henry II Jasomirgott</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bavarian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Marcha Orientalis, Ostarrîchi, Bavarian Eastern March</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1066&amp;focus=margraviate-of-austria" aria-label="Margraviate of Austria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5036 491 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Margraviate of Austria lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5356 747L5279 723M5356 747L5229 694M5356 747L5381 747"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5279 723h0M5229 694h0M5381 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5356 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5356 747h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Melk. <a href="/atlas?year=1066&amp;focus=margraviate-of-austria">On the atlas 